      "message": "So I kinda left it as an open issue for discussion (which obviously I forgot to tag here, apologies for that!)\nThe final solution should be using a way to apply the workaround (as of set of new dma-ops) selectively: so there are two options here (both based on vendor hooks):\n\n1: Add a vendor hook here i.e. trace_android_vh_set_dma_ops(dev) which vendor modules can register for and then use a fact that the arm64_noalias_setup_dma_ops is being exported and call that one directly from the module, seems like the cleanest way to handle it though there is no guarantee the registration for that hook will happen on time\n\n2: Add a vendor hook directly in arm64_noalias_setup_dma_ops which means additional steps to check whatever the workaround is to be applied or not (so there needs to be a feedback from vendor hook saying yes/no). \n\nIf we go for option 2 then yes, we can probably consider renaming that function to be more implying its purpose, though I am leaning towards option 1, which would save some cycles and would leave the workaround unused unless explicitly requested. \nIt is still slightly against the overall idea for CPU erratum (which should preferably work the other way round: apply unless smth claims (for whatever reason) that it is not needed).",
